  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CDT, temperatures across the area are in the upper \n40s to low 50s with winds out of the east to southeast at less \nthan 10 mph. Some light showers may pop up between now and mid \nmorning across much of the forecast area. Little accumulation is\nexpected around and west of the Missouri River with up to a \ntenth expected east of the Missouri River. Some fog may also be \npossible mainly across the western half of the forecast area, \nbut with the lack of precipitation, this could end up being low \nstratus with not much of a reduction of visibility. Models are \nshowing another round of light showers or drizzle moving in from\nthe south this evening into Sunday morning, again, only light \naccumulations are expected.\n\nThe main story for the forecast remains the early work week \nprecipitation. With an incoming low pressure system, we have \nwidespread chances of at least 70% for about 24 hours. PWATs are \nstill in the 1 to 1.25 inch range and still in that 99th or higher \nNAEFS percentile. The NBM chances for accumulations higher than two \ninches in 24 hours ending Tuesday morning is 50 to 75% around and \neast of a line from Leola to Faulkton to Chamberlain. These totals \nseem to be rather high and have increased dramatically in the last \n24 hours. However, ensembles do show a 25th to 75th percentile range \nof 1 to 3 inches. Would not be surprised if totals come back down \ntowards an inch in the next few days. Overall confidence in totals \nis low at this time.\n\nTemperatures through Sunday are still expected to be 10-15 degrees \nabove normal, with highs mainly in the 60s. Colder air moves in \nbehind the low and front Monday and will bring highs down to around \nnormal for this time of year. Monday also looks to be quite windy \nwith gusts of at least 35 to 40 mph for the majority of the forecast \narea, some locally higher gusts of 45 to 50 may also be possible for \ncentral SD. Keep an eye on this in the next few days. These stronger \ngusts look to continue overnight and into Tuesday.",
